<section class="hero"><div class="wrap hero-in"><div>
<p class="kick">Fabric &middot; Fit &middot; Care</p>
<h1>Your clothes are only as good as the cloth.</h1>
<p class="lead">Fabrica is an independent guide to what your clothes are made of. Decode a care label, convert a size properly and see how a blend will behave before you buy it.</p>
<p class="hero-cta"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Try the three tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="fabric-guide.html">Read the fabric guide</a></p></div>
<figure class="hero-fig"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1687226426209-0e5d2143c0d1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&q=75" alt="A rack of white shirts hanging in front of a mirror" width="900" height="1125" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Three free tools &middot; no sign-up</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<div class="wrap"><div class="rail">
<div><b>8</b><span>fibres decoded</span></div><div><b>4</b><span>sizing systems</span></div><div><b>0</b><span>sponsored picks</span></div><div><b>100%</b><span>free to use</span></div></div></div>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Where to start</p><h2>Four things worth knowing</h2></div><a href="fabric-guide.html">Full fabric guide &rarr;</a></div>
<div class="num-grid">
<div class="num"><span>01</span><h3>Read the composition first</h3><p>The fibre list in the seam tells you more about a garment than the photo, the price or the brand ever will.</p></div>
<div class="num"><span>02</span><h3>Measure, do not guess</h3><p>Sizes are a brand&#8217;s opinion. A tape measure is a fact. Keep three numbers written down and shop by those.</p></div>
<div class="num"><span>03</span><h3>Wash cooler, wash less</h3><p>Most damage happens in the machine, not on your back. Lower heat and fewer cycles buy you years.</p></div>
<div class="num"><span>04</span><h3>Match fibre to purpose</h3><p>Linen for heat, wool for cold and damp, cotton for everything in between, synthetics where sweat or stretch matter.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Work it out in seconds</h2></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="care"><h3>Fabric Care Decoder</h3><p>Pick a fibre and see how to wash, dry and press it, plus the mistakes that ruin it.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="fab">Main fibre</label><select id="fab"><option value="cotton">Cotton</option><option value="linen">Linen</option><option value="wool">Wool</option><option value="silk">Silk</option><option value="viscose">Viscose / rayon</option><option value="polyester">Polyester</option><option value="denim">Denim (cotton twill)</option><option value="cashmere">Cashmere</option></select></div>
<p style="font-size:.9rem;color:#C9C3B8;margin:0">If your label lists several fibres, choose the one with the highest percentage, then follow the gentlest advice of the fibres present.</p></div>
<div class="out" id="careOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="size"><h3>Size Converter</h3><p>Turn a US size into UK, EU and IT, with the body measurement it is cut for.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="szType">Garment</label><select id="szType"><option value="tops">Tops, shirts and dresses</option><option value="bottoms">Trousers and skirts</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="szUs">US size</label><select id="szUs"><option value="0">US 0</option><option value="1">US 2</option><option value="2">US 4</option><option value="3" selected>US 6</option><option value="4">US 8</option><option value="5">US 10</option><option value="6">US 12</option><option value="7">US 14</option><option value="8">US 16</option></select></div></div>
<div class="out" id="szOut"></div></div></div>
<figure style="margin:0 0 26px"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1680835099030-9c7532f744f1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=75" alt="A room filled with mannequins and sewing machines" width="1200" height="500" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:12/5;object-fit:cover;width:100%"></figure>
<div class="tool" id="blend"><h3>Blend Builder</h3><p>Mix two fibres and see how the cloth is likely to breathe, crease, stretch and last.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="f1">First fibre</label><select id="f1"><option value="cotton" selected>Cotton</option><option value="linen">Linen</option><option value="wool">Wool</option><option value="silk">Silk</option><option value="viscose">Viscose</option><option value="polyester">Polyester</option><option value="elastane">Elastane</option><option value="nylon">Nylon</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="f2">Second fibre</label><select id="f2"><option value="cotton">Cotton</option><option value="linen">Linen</option><option value="wool">Wool</option><option value="silk">Silk</option><option value="viscose">Viscose</option><option value="polyester">Polyester</option><option value="elastane" selected>Elastane</option><option value="nylon">Nylon</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="mix">Share of the first fibre: <span id="mixLab"></span></label><input class="rng" id="mix" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="95"></div></div>
<div class="out" id="blOut"></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="band"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1664333570948-cb94f2985827?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=75" alt="" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
<div class="wrap"><p class="kick" style="color:#E6C9A8">The short version</p><h2>Buy for the cloth, not the label</h2>
<p>A well-made garment in the wrong fibre will still disappoint you. Learn to read the composition line and half of your shopping mistakes disappear.</p>
<p style="margin-top:1.4em"><a class="btn" style="background:#C2663F;color:#fff" href="fit-and-sizing-guide.html">Read the fit guide</a></p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides, start to finish</h2></div></div>
<div class="cards">
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1770196009760-bead9eb10514?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A person inspecting fabric in a workroom" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide one</p><h3>The Fabric Guide</h3><p>Every common fibre explained: how it feels, how it wears, what it hates and when to choose it. Plus weave, weight and what GSM really means.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="fabric-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769107805528-964f4de0e342?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="Neutral dresses on a display rack" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide two</p><h3>Fit &amp; Sizing Guide</h3><p>How to measure yourself, how ease works, why vanity sizing exists and how to shop across UK, EU and US charts without guessing.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="fit-and-sizing-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><div><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>Asked all the time</h2></div></div>
<div class="faq"><details><summary>How do I know what my clothes are made of?</summary><p>Look for the small composition label, usually in a side seam or under the care label. It lists every fibre by percentage, highest first. That single line tells you how the garment will wash, crease, stretch and wear.</p></details><details><summary>Is natural fabric always better than synthetic?</summary><p>No. Natural fibres like cotton, linen and wool breathe well and age gracefully. Synthetics resist creasing, dry quickly and add stretch. The right choice depends on what the garment has to do: a summer shirt and a running top want opposite things.</p></details><details><summary>Why does the same size fit differently in each shop?</summary><p>There is no legal standard for clothing sizes. Each brand builds its patterns on its own fit model and adjusts the ease it adds. That is why a measurement in inches or centimetres is far more useful than a number on a label.</p></details><details><summary>How often should I actually wash my clothes?</summary><p>Far less than most people do. Wool and denim want airing, not washing. Shirts and underwear worn next to the skin need washing after each wear. Washing is the main thing that wears clothes out, so a shorter, cooler cycle usually serves you better.</p></details><details><summary>What does a blend percentage really tell me?</summary><p>It tells you which fibre leads. A blend behaves roughly like a weighted average of its parts, but it must be cared for like the most delicate fibre in the mix. Even five per cent elastane changes how a garment recovers its shape.</p></details></div></div></section>
<section class="nl"><div class="wrap"><div><p class="kick" style="color:#1D2440">Newsletter</p><h2>The Fabrica Thread</h2>
<p>One short email a month: a fibre explained, a care mistake worth avoiding and whatever we have added to the guides. No spam, unsubscribe any time.</p></div>
<div><p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@fabrica.com?subject=The%20Fabrica%20Thread&body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></p><p style="font-size:.9rem;margin:0">Your email app opens with the sign-up ready to send, so nothing is stored here until you send it.</p></div></div></section>
